  "overview": "The Leonardo looks like an Uno at a glance — same size, same header layout — but runs a different chip: the ATmega32U4, which has USB built directly into the microcontroller instead of relying on a second chip to translate USB to serial. The practical upshot is that a Leonardo can pretend to be a USB keyboard, mouse, or generic HID device, which the Uno simply cannot do. The tradeoff is a genuinely different upload/reset behavior that trips up people expecting Uno-like behavior: opening the Serial Monitor doesn't reset the board, and code needs to explicitly wait for the USB connection before printing.",
  "simpleSummary": "An Arduino that can pretend to be a keyboard or mouse to your computer.",
